body.text:
(prathamam āhnikam)
[saṃśayaparīkṣāprakaraṇam]
2.1.1: samānānekadharmādhyavasāyāt anyataradharmādhyavasāyāt vā na saṃśayaḥ
{saṃśaya-}
2.1.2: viprattipattyavyavasthādhyavasāyāt ca
{saṃśaya-}
2.1.3: viprattipattau ca saṃprattipatteḥ
{saṃśaya-}
2.1.4: avyavasthā ātmani vyavasthitatvāt ca avyavasthāyāḥ
{saṃśaya-}
2.1.5: tathā atyantasaṃśayaḥ taddharmasātatyopapatteḥ
{saṃśaya-}
2.1.6: yathoktādhyavasāyāt eva tadviśeṣāpekṣāt saṃśaye na asaṃśayaḥ na atyantasaṃśayaḥ vā
{saṃśaya-}
2.1.7: yatra saṃśayaḥ tatra evam uttarottaraprasaṅgaḥ
{saṃśaya-}
[pramāṇasāmānyaparīkṣāprakaraṇam]
2.1.8: pratyakṣādīnām aprāmāṇyaṃ traikālyāsiddheḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.9: pūrvaṃ hi pramāṇasiddhau na indriyārthasannikarṣāt pratyakṣotpattiḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.10: paścātsiddhau na pramāṇebhyaḥ prameyasiddhiḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.11: yugapatsiddhau pratyarthaniyatatvāt kramavṛttitvābhāvaḥ buddhīnām
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.12: traikālyāsiddheḥ pratiṣedhānupapattiḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.13: sarvapramāṇapratiṣedhāt ca pratiṣedhānupapattiḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.14: tatprāmāṇye vā na sarvapramāṇavipratiṣedhaḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.15: traikālyāpratiṣedhaḥ ca śabdāt ātodyasiddhivat tatsiddheḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.16: prameyā ca tulāprāmāṇyavat
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.17: pramāṇataḥ siddheḥ pramāṇānām pramāṇāntarasiddhiprasaṅgaḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.18: tadvinivṛtteḥ vā pramāṇasiddhivat prameyasiddhiḥ
{pūrvapakṣasūtram}
2.1.19: na, pradīpaprakāśasiddhivat tatsiddheḥ
{siddhāntalakṣaṇam}
2.1.20: kvacit nivṛttidarśanāt anivṛttidarśanāt ca kvacit anekāntaḥ
{siddhāntalakṣaṇam}
[pratyakṣaparīkṣāprakaraṇām]
2.1.21: pratyakṣalakṣaṇānupapattiḥ asamagravacanāt
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-}
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇaparīkṣā}
2.1.22: na ātmamanasoḥ sannikarṣābhāve pratyakṣotpattiḥ
(do)
2.1.23: digdeśakālākāśeṣu api evaṃ prasaṅgaḥ
(do)
2.1.24: jñānaliṅgatvāt ātmanaḥ na anavarodhaḥ
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-siddhānta-sūtram}
2.1.25: tadayaugapadyaliṅgatvāt ca na manasaḥ
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.26: pratyakṣanimittatvāt ca indriyārthayoḥ sannikarṣasya svaśabdena vacanam
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.27: suptavyāsaktamanasāṃ ca indriyārthayoḥ sannikarṣanimittatvāt
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.28: taiḥ ca apadeśaḥ jñānaviśeṣāṇām
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.29: vyāhatatvāt ahetuḥ
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.30: na arthaviśeṣaprābalyāt
{pratyakṣalakṣaṇa-siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
(First lesson)
[Section on the examination of doubt (<s>saṃśaya</s>)]
2.1.1: There is no doubt (<s>saṃśaya</s>) from the ascertainment of common or multiple properties, or from the ascertainment of one of the properties.
{doubt-}
2.1.2: And [doubt arises] from the ascertainment of conflicting views and unsettled positions.
{doubt-}
2.1.3: And [this is valid] because in conflicting views, there is also correct view.
{doubt-}
2.1.4: Unsettledness [cannot exist as such], because of being established in itself; and [this applies to the argument from] unsettledness.
{doubt-}
2.1.5: Likewise, there cannot be perpetual doubt, because of the possibility of the continuity of that property.
{doubt-}
2.1.6: By the above-described ascertainment alone, which depends on the specific [context], in a case of doubt, there is neither absence of doubt nor perpetual doubt.
{doubt-}
2.1.7: Wherever there is doubt, there is in the same way the absurdity of successive further [doubts].
{doubt-}
[Section on the general examination of means of valid knowledge (<s>pramāṇa</s>)]
2.1.8: The invalidity of perception (<s>pratyakṣa</s>) and the other [<s>pramāṇas</s>] follows because [their establishment is] impossible in the three times.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.9: For if [<s>pramāṇas</s>] are established first, then perception does not arise from the contact of sense organ and object.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.10: If established afterward, there is no establishment of the knowable (<s>prameya</s>) from the <s>pramāṇas</s>.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.11: If established simultaneously, since [cognitions] are restricted to individual objects, there is no possibility of the successive occurrence of cognitions.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.12: Because of the impossibility in the three times, the negation [of <s>pramāṇas</s>] is untenable.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.13: And because of the negation of all <s>pramāṇas</s>, the negation [itself] is untenable.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.14: Or if that [negation] itself is valid, then there is no contradiction of all <s>pramāṇas</s>.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.15: And the impossibility in the three times does not [hold], [for <s>pramāṇas</s> are established] from verbal testimony (<s>śabda</s>), like the establishment of [the sound of] a musical instrument; because of its establishment.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.16: And [<s>pramāṇas</s>] are [themselves] to be measured, like the validity of a balance.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.17: Since [<s>pramāṇas</s>] are established by a <s>pramāṇa</s>, there arises the absurdity that <s>pramāṇas</s> require establishment by another <s>pramāṇa</s>.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.18: Or, from the cessation of that [regress], the establishment of the knowable (<s>prameya</s>) is just like the establishment of the <s>pramāṇa</s> [itself].
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.19: No, because of their establishment like the establishment of the illumination of a lamp.
{established doctrine (<s>siddhānta</s>)}
2.1.20: Because in some cases cessation is observed and in some cases non-cessation, [the argument is] inconclusive.
{established doctrine}
[Section on the examination of perception (<s>pratyakṣa</s>)]
2.1.21: The definition of perception (<s>pratyakṣa</s>) is untenable because of being an incomplete statement.
{examination of the definition of perception}
2.1.22: In the absence of contact between the self (<s>ātman</s>) and the mind (<s>manas</s>), perception does not arise.
(do)
2.1.23: The same absurdity [would apply] in the case of direction, place, time, and space (<s>ākāśa</s>) as well.
(do)
2.1.24: Because the self (<s>ātman</s>) is inferred through the mark of knowledge, there is no exclusion [of the self from the definition].
{established conclusion}
2.1.25: And because the mind (<s>manas</s>) is inferred through the mark of non-simultaneity [of cognitions], [it is not excluded either].
{established conclusion}
2.1.26: Because the contact of sense organ and object is the cause of perception (<s>pratyakṣa</s>), it is stated by its own term.
{established conclusion}
2.1.27: And because, for those who are asleep or whose minds are distracted, the contact of sense organ and object is [still] the instrumental cause [but perception does not arise, the definition is valid].
{established conclusion}
2.1.28: And by these [criteria], different types of knowledge are designated.
{established conclusion}
2.1.29: Because of being self-contradictory, [the opponent's objection] is not a [valid] reason.
{established conclusion}
2.1.30: Not so, because of the predominance of the specific object.
{established conclusion}

body.text:
2.1.31: pratyakṣam anumānam ekadeśagrahaṇāt upalabdheḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.32: na, pratyakṣeṇa yāvat tāvat api upalambhāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.33: na caikadeśopalabdhiravayavisadbhāvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
[prasaṅgopāttā avayaviparīkṣā]
2.1.34: sādhyatvāt avayavini sandehaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.35: sarvāgrahaṇam avayavyasiddheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.36: dhāraṇākarṣaṇopapatteḥ ca
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.37: senāvanavat grahaṇam iti cet na atīndriyatvāt aṇūnām
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
2.1.31: Perception is [merely] inference, because of the apprehension of only a part [of the object].
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.32: No, because of apprehending as much as is [available] through perception.
{established conclusion}
2.1.33: And the apprehension of a part is not [merely partial], because of the existence of parts [in the whole].
{established conclusion}
[Incidental examination of the whole (<s>avayavin</s>)]
2.1.34: There is doubt about the whole (<s>avayavin</s>) because it is to be proved.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.35: There is no complete apprehension [of all parts], because the whole is not established.
{established conclusion}
2.1.36: And because of the possibility of holding and pulling [which require a whole].
{established conclusion}
2.1.37: If [one says] apprehension [of the whole] is like [the apprehension of] an army or a forest, no, because atoms are beyond the senses.
{established conclusion}

body.text:
2.1.38: rodhopaghātasādṛśyebhyaḥ vyabhicārāt anumānam apramāṇam
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.39: na, ekadeśatrāsasādṛśyebhyaḥ arthāntarabhāvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
{vartamānakālaparīkṣā}
2.1.40: vartamānābhāvaḥ, patataḥ patitapatitavyakālopapatteḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.41: tayoḥ api abhāvaḥ vartamānābhāve, tadapekṣatvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.42: na atītānāgatayoḥ itaretarāpekṣā siddhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.43: vartamānābhāve sarvāgrahaṇaṃ pratyakṣānupapatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.44: kṛtatākarttavyatopapatteḥ tu ubhayathā grahaṇam
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
2.1.38: Inference is not a valid means of knowledge (<s>pramāṇa</s>) because of deviation (<s>vyabhicāra</s>) in [cases of] obstruction, damage, and similarity.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.39: No, because these—fright at a part, and similarity—are different things [from the inferential mark itself].
{established conclusion}
{Examination of the present time}
2.1.40: The present does not exist, because the time of what is falling admits of 'has fallen' and 'is yet to fall.'
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.41: In the absence of the present, those two (past and future) also do not exist, because they depend on it.
{established conclusion}
2.1.42: The establishment of past and future is not through mutual dependence on each other.
{established conclusion}
2.1.43: In the absence of the present, there is no complete apprehension, because perception would be impossible.
{established conclusion}
2.1.44: But because of the possibility of what has been done (<s>kṛtatā</s>) and what is to be done (<s>kartavyatā</s>), apprehension is of both kinds.
{established conclusion}

body.text:
2.1.45: atyantaprāyaikadeśasādharmyāt upamānāsiddhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.46: prasiddhasādharmyāt upamānasiddheḥ yathoktadoṣānupapattiḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.47: pratyakṣeṇa apratyakṣasiddheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.48: na apratyakṣe gavaye pramāṇārtham upamānasya paśyāmaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.49: tathā iti upasaṃhārāt upamānasiddheḥ na aviśeṣaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
[śabdasāmānyaparīkṣāprakaraṇam]
2.1.50: śabdaḥ anumānam arthasya anupalabdheḥ anumeyatvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.51: upalabdheḥ advipravṛttitvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.52: sambandhāt ca
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.1.53: āptopadeśasāmarthyāt śabdāt arthasampratyayaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.54: pūraṇapradāhapāṭanānupalabdheḥ ca sambandhābhāvaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.55: śabdārthavyavasthānāt apratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.56: na, sāmayikatvāt śabdārthasampratyayasya
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.57: jātiviśeṣe ca aniyamāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
[śabdaviśeṣaparīkṣāprakaraṇam]
2.1.58: tadaprāmāṇyam anṛtavyāghātapunaruktadoṣebhyaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.59: na, karmakartṛsādhanavaiguṇyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.60: abhyupetya kālabhede doṣavacanāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.61: anuvādopapatteḥ ca
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.62: vākyavibhāgasya ca arthagrahaṇāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.63: vidhyarthavādānuvādavacanaviniyogāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.64: vidhiḥ vidhāyakaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.65: stutiḥ nindā parakṛtiḥ purākalpaḥ iti arthavādaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
{arthavāda-bhedaḥ}
2.1.66: vidhivihitasya anuvacanam anuvādaḥ
{arthavāda-}
2.1.67: na anuvādapunaruktayoḥ viśeṣaḥ, śabdābhyāsopapatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.68: śīghrataragamanopadeśavat abhyāsāt na aviśeṣaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.1.69: mantrāyurvedaprāmāṇyavat ca tatprāmāṇyam, āptaprāmāṇyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
2.1.45: <s>Upamāna</s> (analogy) is not established because of [objections from] complete, predominant, and partial similarity.
{established conclusion}
2.1.46: Because of the establishment of <s>upamāna</s> through well-known similarity, the above-stated faults do not apply.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.47: Because the imperceptible is established through the perceptible.
{established conclusion}
2.1.48: We do not see that <s>upamāna</s> serves as a means of knowledge for an imperceptible <s>gavaya</s> (wild ox).
{established conclusion}
2.1.49: Because of the subsumption 'it is like that,' the establishment of <s>upamāna</s> is not without distinction.
{established conclusion}
[Section on the general examination of verbal testimony (<s>śabda</s>)]
2.1.50: Verbal testimony (<s>śabda</s>) is [merely] inference, because the object is not apprehended [directly] and because it is to be inferred.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.51: [This is so] because of apprehension and because it does not lead to two [different] actions.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.52: And because of [the word-meaning] relation.
{opponent's sūtra}
2.1.53: The comprehension of meaning from verbal testimony (<s>śabda</s>) arises from the capacity of the instruction of a trustworthy person (<s>āpta</s>).
{established conclusion}
2.1.54: And because filling, burning, and splitting are not apprehended [in words], the relation [between word and meaning is not natural].
{established conclusion}
2.1.55: Because of the conventional fixity of words and meanings, there is no negation [of verbal testimony].
{established conclusion}
2.1.56: No, because the comprehension of word-meaning is conventional (<s>sāmayika</s>).
{established conclusion}
2.1.57: And because there is no fixed rule regarding universal and particular.
{established conclusion}
[Section on the specific examination of verbal testimony]
2.1.58: The invalidity of that [Vedic testimony] follows from the faults of falsehood, self-contradiction, and tautology.
{established conclusion}
2.1.59: No, because of the deficiency in action, agent, or instrument [rather than in the scripture itself].
{established conclusion}
2.1.60: Because, having accepted [the validity], the statement of fault [applies] to a different time.
{established conclusion}
2.1.61: And because of the possibility of restatement (<s>anuvāda</s>).
{established conclusion}
2.1.62: And because meaning is grasped from the division of sentences.
{established conclusion}
2.1.63: Because of the distribution of sentences into injunction (<s>vidhi</s>), explanatory passage (<s>arthavāda</s>), restatement (<s>anuvāda</s>), and application (<s>viniyoga</s>).
{established conclusion}
2.1.64: An injunction (<s>vidhi</s>) is that which prescribes.
{established conclusion}
2.1.65: Praise, censure, narrative of another's deed, and ancient legend—these constitute explanatory passage (<s>arthavāda</s>).
{established conclusion}
{types of explanatory passage}
2.1.66: The re-statement of what has been enjoined by an injunction is restatement (<s>anuvāda</s>).
{explanatory passage-}
2.1.67: There is no difference between restatement (<s>anuvāda</s>) and tautology (<s>punarukta</s>), because of the possibility of verbal repetition.
{established conclusion}
2.1.68: Just as the instruction 'go faster'—through repetition there is no absence of distinction.
{established conclusion}
2.1.69: And its validity, like the validity of <s>mantras</s> and <s>Āyurveda</s>, follows from the validity of trustworthy persons (<s>āpta</s>).
{established conclusion}

body.text:
2.2.1: na catuṣṭvam, aitihyārthāpattisambhavābhāvaprāmāṇyāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.2.2: śabde aitihyānarthāntarabhāvāt anumāne arthāpattisambhavābhāvānarthāntara bhāvāt ca apratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.2.3: arthāpattiḥ apramāṇam anaikāntikatvāt
{arthāpattiprāmāṇyaparīkṣā}
2.2.4: anarthāpattau arthāpattyabhimānāt
{nānaikāntikatvamarthāpatteḥ}
2.2.5: pratiṣedhāprāmāṇyaṃ ca anaikāntikatvāt
(-)
2.2.6: tatprāmāṇye vā na arthāpattyaprāmāṇyam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
[abhāvaprāmāṇyasiddhiḥ - avāntaraprakaraṇam]
2.2.7: na abhāvaprāmāṇyaṃ prameyāsiddheḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.2.8: lakṣiteṣvalakṣaṇalakṣitatvāt alakṣitānāṃ tatprameyasiddheḥ
{udāharaṇa sūtra}
2.2.9: asati arthe na abhāvaḥ iti cet na, anyalakṣaṇopapatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.2.10: tatsiddheḥ alakṣiteṣu ahetuḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
2.2.11: na, lakṣaṇāvasthitāpekṣasiddheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.2.12: prāk utpatteḥ abhāvopapatteḥ ca
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
2.2.1: [The means of valid knowledge] are not four, because of the validity of <s>aitihya</s> (tradition), <s>arthāpatti</s> (presumption), <s>sambhava</s> (inclusion), and <s>abhāva</s> (non-existence). {objection sūtra}
2.2.2: In the case of verbal testimony (<s>śabda</s>), because tradition is not different [from it]; in the case of inference (<s>anumāna</s>), because presumption, inclusion, and non-existence are not different [from it] — there is no refutation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.3: <s>Arthāpatti</s> (presumption) is not a [separate] valid means of knowledge, because of its inconclusiveness (<s>anaikāntikatva</s>). {examination of the validity of presumption}
2.2.4: Because of the assumption of presumption even where there is no [actual] presumption [needed]. {Presumption is not inconclusive}
2.2.5: And the refutation [of presumption] too is not valid, because of inconclusiveness.
2.2.6: Or, if that refutation is valid, then the invalidity of presumption does not [follow]. {established conclusion sūtra}
[Establishment of the validity of non-existence as a means of knowledge — sub-section]
2.2.7: <s>Abhāva</s> (non-existence) is not a [separate] valid means of knowledge, because the object to be known is not established. {objection sūtra}
2.2.8: Because among things that are characterized [as existent], things that are characterized by the absence of [those] characteristics are [also] known — therefore the object of that [means of knowledge] is established. {illustration sūtra}
2.2.9: If [one objects] that when the object does not exist, there is no non-existence — no, because another characterization is justified. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.10: Since that [characterization] is established, [non-existence] is not a [valid] reason regarding uncharacterized [things]. {objection sūtra}
2.2.11: No, because the establishment depends on the persistence of characterization. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.12: And because the justification of non-existence [obtains] prior to origination. {established conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
2.2.13: ādimatvāt aindriyakatvāt kṛtakavat upacārāt ca
{śabdānityatva-pratipādana-sūtra}
2.2.14: na, ghaṭābhāvasāmānyanityatvāt nityeṣu api anityavat upacārāt ca
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.2.15: tattvabhāktayoḥ nānātvavibhāgāt avyabhicāraḥ
{śabdānityatva-pratipādana-sūtra}
{siddhānta-sūtra}
2.2.16: santānānumānaviśeṣaṇāt
