body.translated_text:
2.2.13: Because of having a beginning (<s>ādimatva</s>), because of being sensory (<s>aindriyakatva</s>), because of figurative usage like [that for] produced things. {sūtra establishing the impermanence of sound}
2.2.14: No, because of the permanence of the non-existence of a pot as a general [principle]; and because of figurative usage as impermanent even regarding permanent [things]. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.15: Because of the distinction and division of the literal and the figurative, there is no deviation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.16: Because of the distinguishing mark of inference from continuity. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.17: Because the causal substance is denoted by the word for the part (<s>pradeśa</s>). {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.18: Because of non-apprehension prior to utterance, and because of the non-apprehension of an obstruction. {sūtra establishing impermanence of sound}
2.2.19: From the non-apprehension of that [obstruction], because of non-perception, the justification of obstruction [obtains]. {sūtra establishing impermanence of sound}
2.2.20: From non-perception, because of the [actual] existence of non-apprehension, there is no unjustifiability of obstruction from non-perception. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.21: Because non-apprehension is of the nature of non-perception, [the reason of] non-apprehension is inconclusive. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.22: Because of intangibility (<s>asparśatva</s>). {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.23: No, because of the impermanence of action (<s>karman</s>). {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.24: No, because of the permanence of atoms (<s>aṇu</s>). {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.25: Because of [being the object of] instruction (<s>sampradāna</s>). {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.26: Because of the non-apprehension of that [sound] in the interval, [the reason] is inconclusive. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.27: From teaching (<s>adhyāpana</s>), there is no refutation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.28: From teaching [being applicable to] either of the two positions, there is no refutation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.29: Because of repetition (<s>abhyāsa</s>). {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.30: No, because of figurative usage of repetition even when there is otherness. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.31: That which is other than another — because of [its] non-otherness — is non-other; thus the absence of otherness. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.32: In the absence of that [otherness], there is no non-otherness either, because both are established in mutual dependence. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.33: Because of the non-apprehension of a cause of destruction. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.34: Because of the non-apprehension of a cause of non-hearing, there would follow constant hearing. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.35: And because of the non-existence of non-apprehension when [the object] is being apprehended, [the argument] is without ground. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.36: Because of the intimate connection with the hand and other [instruments], in the absence of sound there is no non-apprehension. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.37: And because of the non-apprehension of a cause of destruction, if [the sound] persists, there would follow its permanence. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.38: Because of intangibility, there is no refutation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.39: And because of the justification of a different case-ending in a compound. {established conclusion sūtra}

