body.translated_text:
2.2.40: Doubt [arises] because of the teaching of modification and substitution. {objection sūtra}
2.2.41: Because of the increase of the modification when the original form increases. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.42: Because of the apprehension of less, equal, and more among modifications, [the reason] is inconclusive. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.43: Because of the absence of even a twofold reason, the example is non-probative. {illustration sūtra}
2.2.44: No, because of the variety of modification among things of unequal original form. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.45: Like the inequality of material modification, the modification of phonemes (<s>varṇa</s>) is deficient. {objection sūtra}
2.2.46: No, because the properties of modification are unjustified. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.47: Because things that have attained modification do not return [to their original form]. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.48: Because of the return of gold and the like, [the reason] is inconclusive. {objection sūtra}
2.2.49: No, because the modifications of gold do not deviate from the nature of gold. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.50: Because of the non-deviation from phoneme-ness of phonemic modifications, there is no refutation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.51: The properties belong to the possessor of the universal, not to the universal [itself]. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.52: Because there is no modification if [the universal is] permanent, and because of non-persistence if [it is] impermanent. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.53: Because permanent [entities] are supersensible, and because of the variety of their properties, there is no refutation of the modifications of phonemes. {objection sūtra}
2.2.54: And if [phonemes] are non-persistent, the justification of their modification [obtains], just as the apprehension of phonemes [obtains]. {objection sūtra}
2.2.55: Because, if having the property of modification, permanence would be absent; and because the justification of modification at another time — there is no refutation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.56: Because there is no restriction of the original form. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.57: In the case of non-restriction, because of restriction, there is no non-restriction. {objection sūtra}
2.2.58: Because of the contradiction between restriction and non-restriction, and because of restriction in the case of non-restriction — there is no refutation. {established conclusion sūtra}
2.2.59: But phonemic modifications are justified through the acquisition of another quality, suppression, diminution, increase, and combination. {established conclusion sūtra}

