body.translated_text:
3.1.15: [Objection:] No [distinction between <s>ātman</s> and <s>manas</s>], because the means of apprehending the self are possible in the mind.
{Objection}
3.1.16: Since for the knower the instrument of knowledge is established, [the difference between <s>ātman</s> and <s>manas</s>] is merely a difference of designation.
{Established position}
3.1.17: And the restriction [of <s>manas</s> to one cognition at a time] is without [corresponding] inference [for the <s>ātman</s>].
{Established position}
[Section: Examination of the eternality of the <s>ātman</s> (18-26)]
3.1.18: Because of the apprehension of joy, fear, and grief in the newborn, through the connection with memory of what was practiced in a previous [life].
{Established position}
3.1.19: [Objection:] Like the changes of opening and closing in lotuses and the like, that [joy etc.] is [merely] a modification of that [body].
{Objection}
3.1.20: No, because the modifications of the five elements are caused by the conditions of heat, cold, and rain.
{Established position}
3.1.21: Because of the desire for the breast [in the newborn], which is due to the practice of eating performed in the previous [life].
{Established position}
3.1.22: [Objection:] That approach [of the infant to the breast] is like the approach of iron to a magnet.
{Objection}
3.1.23: No, because there is no such movement in other [cases].
{Established position}
3.1.24: Because no birth of one who is free from attachment is observed.
{Established position}
3.1.25: [Objection:] That origination [of attachment] is like the origination of a substance possessing qualities.
{Objection}
3.1.26: No, because desire and the like are caused by volition (<s>saṅkalpa</s>).
{Established position}

