{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.43: abhivyaktau ca abhibhavāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.44: naktañcaranayanaraśmidarśanāt ca
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.45: aprāpyagrahaṇaṃ kācābhrapaṭalasphaṭikāntaritopalabdheḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.46: kuḍyāntaritānupalabdheḥ apratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.47: apratīghātāt sannikarṣopapattiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.48: ādityaraśmeḥ sphaṭikāntarite api dāhye avighātāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.49: na itaretaradharmaprasaṅgāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.50: ādarśodakayoḥ prasādasvābhāvyāt rūpopalabdhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.51: dṛṣṭānumitānāṃ hi niyogapratiṣedhānupapattiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.1.32: When the dark-spotted [antelope, i.e., visible object] is present, there is perception; and perception also [occurs] separately [when it is absent] — hence doubt [about whether the senses extend to the object or not].
{Established position}
3.1.33: Because [the eye grasps] the large and the small [simultaneously].
{Refutation of the <s>Sāṃkhya</s> view}
3.1.34: That grasping [of distant objects] is due to the specific contact of rays with the object.
{Established position}
3.1.35: That is a groundless [argument], because of the non-perception of those [rays].
{Established position}
3.1.36: The non-perception of what is inferred through direct perception is not a reason for its non-existence.
{Established position}
3.1.37: And the restriction of perception [to specific sense-organs] is due to the differences of substance, quality, and property.
{Established position}
3.1.38: The perception of form [is due to] the inherence in multiple substances and the specificity of form.
{Established position}
3.1.39: And the arrangement of the sense organs, produced by <s>karma</s> (action), is governed by the purpose of the person (<s>puruṣa</s>).
{Established position}
3.1.40: The non-perception of those [rays] is like the non-perception of the light of a meteor at midday.
{Established position}
3.1.41: No, because there is non-perception [of the meteor] also at night.
{Established position}
3.1.42: Since the perception of objects [depends on] the assistance of external light, the non-perception is due to non-manifestation.
{Established position}
3.1.43: And when manifested, [non-perception occurs] due to being overpowered.
{Established position}
3.1.44: And because the eye-rays of nocturnal creatures are observed.
{Established position}
3.1.45: [Objection:] Grasping without contact [is possible], because of the perception [of objects] through glass, clouds, crystal, and the like intervening.
{Objection}
3.1.46: There is no refutation, because of the non-perception [of objects] when a wall intervenes.
{Established position}
3.1.47: Contact is possible because there is no obstruction [by transparent media].
{Established position}
3.1.48: Because a sun-ray burns [an object] even when crystal intervenes, without obstruction.
{Established position}
3.1.49: [Objection:] No, because there would be the undesirable consequence of mutual interchange of properties.
{Objection}
3.1.50: The perception of form in a mirror and water is due to the natural transparency [of those surfaces].
{Established position}
3.1.51: For what is seen and inferred, the impossibility of [unconditionally] enjoining or prohibiting [either mode of perception alone] is established.
{Established position}

body.text:
3.1.52: sthānānyatve nānātvāt avayavinānāsthānatvāt ca saṃśayaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.53: tvak avyatirekāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.54: na yugapat arthānupalabdheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.55: vipratiṣedhāt ca na tvak ekā
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.56: indriyārthapañcatvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.57: na, tadarthabahutvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.58: gandhatvādyavyatirekāt gandhādīnāmapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.59: viṣayatvāvyatirekāt ekatvam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.60: na, buddhilakṣaṇādhiṣṭhāna-gatyākṛti-jātipañcatvebhyaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.61: bhūtaguṇaviśeṣopalabdheḥ tādātmyam
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.1.52: [There is] doubt because of plurality [arising from] difference of location, and because a composite entity does not occupy different locations.
{Established position}
3.1.53: [Objection:] The skin [is just one sense, since it] is not differentiated [from one part to another].
{Objection}
3.1.54: No, because of the non-simultaneous perception of objects.
{Established position}
3.1.55: And the skin is not one, because of mutual contradiction.
{Established position}
3.1.56: Because the sense-objects are five [in number].
{Established position}
3.1.57: [Objection:] No, because the objects of each [sense] are many.
{Objection}
3.1.58: There is no refutation [of the fiveness], because smell etc. are not different [as categories] from smell-ness etc.
{Established position}
3.1.59: [Objection:] [The senses are] one, because of the non-differentiation of being-an-object.
{Objection}
3.1.60: No, because of the five [grounds of differentiation]: cognition, characteristic, substrate, movement, and form/genus.
{Established position}
3.1.61: Because the specific qualities of the [five] elements are perceived, [each sense] is identical in nature [with its corresponding element].
{Established position}

body.text:
3.1.62: gandha-rasa-rūpa-sparśa-śabdānāṃ sparśaparyantāḥ pṛthivyāḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.63: ap-tejo-vāyūnāṃ pūrvaṃ pūrvam apohya ākāśasya uttaraḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.64: na, sarvaguṇānupalabdheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.65: ekaikaśyena uttaraguṇāsadbhāvāt uttarottarāṇāṃ tadanupalabdhiḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.66: viṣṭaṃ hi aparaṃ pareṇa
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.67: na, pārthivāpyayoḥ pratyakṣatvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.68: pūrvapūrvaguṇotkarṣāt tattat pradhānam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.69: taddhyavasthānaṃ tu bhūyastvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.70: saguṇānām indriyabhāvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.71: tena eva tasya agrahaṇāt ca
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.1.72: na, śabdaguṇopalabdheḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.1.73: tadupalabdhiḥ itaretaradravyaguṇavaidharmyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.1.62: Of smell, taste, form, touch, and sound — [the qualities] up to touch belong to earth.
{Established position}
3.1.63: [Objection:] For water, fire, and air, [the qualities are obtained] by removing the preceding ones successively; and for space, the last one [sound alone remains].
{Objection}
3.1.64: No, because not all qualities are perceived [in every element].
{Established position}
3.1.65: [Objection:] Because the successive higher qualities are absent one by one, there is non-perception of those in the successively higher [elements].
{Objection}
3.1.66: [Objection:] For the latter is pervaded by the former.
{Objection}
3.1.67: No, because earth and water are perceptible [even without all five qualities being present].
{Established position}
3.1.68: That in which the preceding qualities are more prominent — that is the predominant [element].
{Established position}
3.1.69: The determination of that [predominance] is based on preponderance.
{Established position}
3.1.70: Because those possessing qualities [serve as] sense organs.
{Established position}
3.1.71: And because by that very [sense organ, the object identical to its own nature] is not grasped.
{Established position}
3.1.72: [Objection:] No, because the quality of sound is perceived [by the ear, which is of the nature of space].
{Objection}
3.1.73: The perception of that [sound] is because of the dissimilarity of the qualities of the substances from each other.
{Established position}

body.text:
3.2.1: karmākāśasādharmyāt saṃśayaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.2: viṣayapratyabhijñānāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.3: sādhyasamatvāt ahetuḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.4: na, yugapat agrahaṇāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.5: apratyabhijñāne ca vināśaprasaṅgaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.6: kramavṛttitvāt ayugapat grahaṇam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.7: apratyabhijñānaṃ ca viṣayāntaravyāsaṅgāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.8: na, gatyabhāvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.9: sphaṭikānyatvābhimānavat tadanyatvābhimānaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.2.1: [There is] doubt [about the permanence of cognition] because of similarity with <s>karma</s> (action) and space.
{Established position}
3.2.2: [Objection: Cognition is permanent] because of the recognition of objects.
{Established position}
3.2.3: [That is] a groundless [argument], because of being equivalent to what is to be proved.
{Established position}
3.2.4: No [permanence], because of the non-simultaneous grasping [of multiple objects].
{Established position}
3.2.5: And if non-recognition [occurred even when the object remained], there would be the undesirable consequence of destruction [of cognition].
{Established position}
3.2.6: The non-simultaneous grasping is because [cognition] operates successively.
{Established position}
3.2.7: And non-recognition is due to preoccupation with a different object.
{Established position}
3.2.8: No, because of the absence of movement [in cognition, it cannot go from one object to another].
{Established position}
3.2.9: The notion of its being different is like the notion of the difference of crystal [from the color reflected in it].
{Established position}

body.text:
3.2.10: sphaṭike api aparāparotpatteḥ kṣaṇikatvāt vyaktīnām ahetuḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.11: niyamahetvabhāvāt yathādarśanam abhyanujñā
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.12: na, utpattivināśakāraṇopalabdheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.13: kṣīravināśe kāraṇānupalabdhivat dadhyutpattivat ca tadutpattiḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.14: liṅgataḥ grahaṇāt na anupalabdhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.15: na payasaḥ pariṇāma-guṇāntaraprādurbhāvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.16: vyūhāntarāt dravyāntarotpattidarśanaṃ pūrvadravyanivṛtteḥ anumānam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.17: kvacit vināśakāraṇānupalabdheḥ kvacit ca upalabdheḥ anekāntaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.2.10: [Even] in a crystal, because of the successive origination of one after another, and because individual manifestations (<s>vyakti</s>) are momentary — [this argument is] groundless.
{Established position}
3.2.11: In the absence of a reason for the restriction [to momentariness], acceptance [should be] in accordance with observation.
{Established position}
3.2.12: No [momentariness], because the causes of origination and destruction are perceived [to be different].
{Established position}
3.2.13: [Objection:] Like the non-perception of the cause of the destruction of milk, and like the origination of curd — so too is that origination [of each new moment].
{Objection}
3.2.14: [The cause] is not unperceived, because it is grasped through inferential marks (<s>liṅga</s>).
{Established position}
3.2.15: No [destruction without cause in the case of milk], because of the transformation of milk — [that is], the manifestation of different qualities.
{Established position}
3.2.16: The observation of the origination of a different substance from a different arrangement [of parts] is an inference for the cessation of the prior substance.
{Established position}
3.2.17: Because in some cases the cause of destruction is not perceived and in other cases it is perceived — [this leads to] inconclusiveness.
{Established position}

body.text:
3.2.18: na indriyārthayoḥ, tadvināśe api jñānāvasthānāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.19: yugapat jñānānupalabdheḥ ca na manasaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.20: tat ātmaguṇatve api tulyam
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.21: indriyaiḥ manasaḥ sannikarṣābhāvāt tadanutpattiḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.22: na, utpattikāraṇānapadeśāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.23: vināśakāraṇānupalabdheḥ ca avasthāne tannityatvaprasaṅgaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.24: anityatvagrahāt buddheḥ buddhyantarāt vināśaḥ śabdavat
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.25: jñānasamavetātmapradeśasannikarṣāt manasaḥ smṛtyutpatteḥ na yugapat utpattiḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.26: na, antaḥ-śarīravṛttitvāt manasaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.27: sādhyatvāt ahetuḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.28: smarataḥ śarīradhāraṇopapatteḥ apratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.29: na, tadāśugatitvānmanasaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.30: na, smaraṇakālāniyamāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.31: ātmapreraṇa-yadṛcchā-jñatābhiḥ ca na saṃyogaviśeṣaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.32: vyāsaktamanasaḥ pādavyathanena saṃyogaviśeṣeṇa samānam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.33: praṇidhānaliṅgādijñānānām ayugapadbhāvāt ayugapatsmaraṇam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.34: jñasya icchādveṣanimittatvāt ārambhanivṛttyoḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.35: talliṅgatvāt icchādveṣayoḥ pārthivādyeṣu apratiṣedhaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.36: paraśvādiṣu ārambhanivṛttidarśanāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.37: niyamāniyamau tu tadviśeṣakau
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.38: yathoktahetutvāt pāratantryāt akṛtābhyāgamāt ca na manasaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.39: pariśeṣāt yathoktahetūpapatteḥ ca
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.40: smaraṇaṃ tu ātmanaḥ jñasvābhāvyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.41: praṇidhāna-nibandhābhyāsa-liṅga-lakṣaṇa-sādṛśya-parigrahāśrayāśrita sambandhānantarya-viyogaikakārya-virodhātiśaya-prāpti-vyavadhāna-sukha-duḥkhecchā dveṣa-bhayārthitva-kriyārāga-dharmādharmanimittebhyaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.2.18: [Cognition] is not [a quality] of the sense organs or their objects, because cognition persists even when those are destroyed.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.19: And [cognition] is not [a quality] of the mind, because cognitions are not apprehended simultaneously.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.20: Even if it is a quality of the self, the same [objection applies].
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.21: Because there is no contact of the mind with the sense organs, that [cognition] is not produced [from the mind alone].
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.22: No, because the cause of production has not been specified [by the objector].
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.23: If [cognition] persists due to the non-apprehension of a cause of destruction, then there would be the absurd consequence of its permanence.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.24: Since cognition is apprehended as impermanent, its destruction is from another cognition—like sound.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.25: Because memory arises from the contact of the mind with the region of the self in which knowledge inheres, there is no simultaneous production.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.26: No, because the mind operates within the body.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.27: Since it remains to be proved, it is not a valid reason.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.28: [This is] not refuted, because the sustaining of the body by one who remembers is possible.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.29: No, because of the swiftness of the mind.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.30: No, because the time of remembering is not fixed.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.31: Through self-impulsion, spontaneity, and being a knower, there is no special conjunction [needed].
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.32: [This is] the same as the special conjunction by the stumbling of the foot of one whose mind is preoccupied.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.33: Because attentive focus, inferential mark, and other cognitions do not arise simultaneously, memory too does not arise simultaneously.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.34: Because desire and aversion of the knower are the causes of activity and cessation.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.35: Because of being their signs, there is no denial of desire and aversion in earthly substances and the like.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.36: Because activity and cessation are observed in axes and the like.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.37: But regulation and non-regulation are what distinguish that [self from instruments].
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.38: Because of having the stated reasons, because of dependence [on another], and because of the absurdity of the unperformed coming to one, [cognition is] not [a quality] of the mind.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.39: By the method of elimination and because the stated reasons apply.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.40: But memory belongs to the self, because it has the nature of a knower.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.41: [Memory arises] from the causes of: attentive focus, association, repetitive practice, inferential mark, characteristic mark, similarity, possession, dependence, the relation of refuge and refugee, proximity, separation, having the same effect, opposition, excess, attainment, separation, pleasure, pain, desire, aversion, fear, need, action, attachment, <s>dharma</s>, and <s>adharma</s>.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
3.2.42: karmānavasthāyigrahaṇāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.43: avyaktagrahaṇam anavasthāyitvāt vidyutsampāte rūpāvyaktagrahaṇavat
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.44: hetūpādānāt pratiṣeddhavyābhyanujñā
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.45: na pradīpārciḥsantatyabhivyaktagrahaṇavat tadgrahaṇam
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.2.42: Because the apprehension of an action does not persist.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.43: The apprehension of what is indistinct does not persist, like the indistinct apprehension of form at the time of a lightning flash.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.44: By the employment of [arguments regarding] the cause, the thing to be refuted is to be granted.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.45: That apprehension is not like the apprehension revealed by the continuity of the flame of a lamp.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
3.2.46: dravye svaguṇaparaguṇopalabdheḥ saṃśayaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.47: yāvaccharīrabhāvitvāt rūpādīnām
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.48: na, pākajaguṇāntarotpatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.49: pratidvandvisiddheḥ pākajānām apratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.50: śarīravyāpitvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.51: na, keśanakhādiṣu anupalabdheḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.52: tvakparyantatvāt śarīrasya keśanakhādiṣu aprasaṅgaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.53: śarīraguṇavaidharmyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.54: na, rūpādīnām itaretaravaidharmyāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.55: aindriyakatvāt rūpādīnām apratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.2.46: There is doubt, because in a substance both its own qualities and the qualities of another are apprehended.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.47: Because form and the like endure as long as the body endures.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.48: No, because different qualities arise through transformation by heat.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.49: There is no denial of things produced by heat, because they are established through counter-evidence.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.50: Because [cognition] pervades the body.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.51: No, because it is not apprehended in hair, nails, and the like.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.52: Because the body extends only to the skin, there is no applicability [of cognition] to hair, nails, and the like.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.53: Because of the dissimilarity with the qualities of the body.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.54: No, because form and the like are mutually dissimilar [among themselves].
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.55: There is no denial of form and the like, because they are perceptible by the senses.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
3.2.56: jñānāyaugapadyāt ekam manaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.57: na, yugapat anekakriyopalabdheḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.58: alātacakradarśanavat tadupalabdhiḥ āśusañcārāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.59: yathoktahetutvāt ca aṇu
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.2.56: Because cognitions do not arise simultaneously, the mind is one.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.57: No, because multiple actions are apprehended simultaneously.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.58: That apprehension is like the appearance of a whirling firebrand as a circle—due to the rapid movement [of the mind].
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.59: And because of having the stated reasons, [the mind] is atomic.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
3.2.60: pūrvakṛtaphalānubandhāt tadutpattiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.61: bhūtebhyaḥ mūrtyupādānavat tadupādānam
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.62: na, sādhyasamatvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.63: na, utpattinimittatvāt mātāpitroḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.64: tathā āhārasya
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.65: prāptau ca aniyamāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.66: śarīrotpattinimittavat saṃyogotpattinimittaṃ karma
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.67: etena aniyamaḥ pratyuktaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.68: tat adṛṣṭakāritam iti cet punaḥ tatprasaṅgaḥ apavarge
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.69: manaḥkarmanimittatvāt ca saṃyogāvyucchedaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.70: nityatvaprasaṅgaḥ ca prāyaṇānupapatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
3.2.71: aṇuśyāmatānityatvavat etat syāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
3.2.72: na, akṛtābhyāgamaprasaṅgāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
3.2.60: From the connection with the fruits of previously performed actions, that [body] is produced.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.61: Just as a form is taken from the elements as material cause, so that [body] is taken [from them].
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.62: No, because it is equivalent to what is to be proved.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.63: No, because the mother and father are the occasioning causes of production.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.64: Likewise food.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.65: And because there is no fixed rule regarding attainment.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.66: Just as the occasioning cause of the production of the body, the occasioning cause of the production of conjunction [of the self with the body] is <s>karma</s> (action).
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.67: By this, the objection regarding the absence of a fixed rule is answered.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.68: If [you say] that [body-production] is caused by the unseen (<s>adṛṣṭa</s>), then the same [consequence] would follow in <s>apavarga</s> (liberation).
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.69: And because the conjunction [of the self with the body] is caused by the mind and <s>karma</s>, there is no cessation of conjunction.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.70: And there would be the absurd consequence of permanence, because death would be impossible.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
3.2.71: This could be like the blackness of the atom, which is impermanent.
{Objection sūtra}
3.2.72: No, because there would be the absurd consequence of the unperformed coming to one.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

4.1.1: Activity is as previously described.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.2: Likewise the faults.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.3: trairāśyaṃ rāgadveṣamohārthāntarbhāvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.4: na, ekapratyanīkabhāvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.5: vyabhicārādahetuḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.6: teṣāṃ mohaḥ pāpīyān, nāmūḍhasyetarotpatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.7: nimittanaimittikabhāvādarthāntarabhāvo doṣebhyaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.8: na doṣalakṣaṇāvarodhānmohasya
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.9: nimittanaimittikopapatteśca tulyajātīyānāmapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.3: The threefold classification [of faults] is due to the inclusion of desire (<s>rāga</s>), aversion (<s>dveṣa</s>), and delusion (<s>moha</s>) within [those categories].
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.4: No, because each has a single opposite.
{Objection sūtra}
4.1.5: Because of deviation, it is not a [valid] reason.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.6: Among these, delusion is the worst, because the others do not arise in one who is not deluded.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.7: Because of the cause-effect relationship, the faults are different from other things.
{Objection sūtra}
4.1.8: No, delusion is [included] because of being encompassed by the definition of a fault.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.9: And because the cause-effect relationship holds, there is no denial of those of the same class.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.10: ātmanityatve pretyabhāvasiddhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.11: vyaktād vyaktānām, pratyakṣaprāmāṇyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.12: na, ghaṭād ghaṭaniṣpatteḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.13: vyaktād ghaṭaniṣpatterapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.10: If the self is permanent, then the existence of an afterlife is established.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.11: [Things arise] from the manifest to the manifest, because of the authority of perception.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.12: No, because a pot is not produced from [another] pot.
{Objection sūtra}
4.1.13: There is no denial of the production of a pot from something manifest.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.14: abhāvād bhāvotpattiḥ, nānupamṛdya prādurbhāvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.15: vyāghātādaprayogaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.16: na, atītānāgatayoḥ kārakaśabdaprayogāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.17: na, vinaṣṭebhyo 'niṣpatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.18: kramanirdeśādapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.14: Existence arises from non-existence, because [things] come into being only after destroying [their prior state].
{Objection sūtra}
4.1.15: Because of [logical] contradiction, [that view] is not to be employed.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.16: No, because causal and verbal usage applies to past and future [entities].
{Objection sūtra}
4.1.17: No, because there is no production from things that are [completely] destroyed.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.18: There is no refutation, because of the sequential indication [of causes and effects].
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.19: īśvaraḥ kāraṇam, puruṣakarmāphalyadarśanāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.20: na, puruṣakarmābhāve phlāniṣpatteḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.21: tatkāritatvād ahetuḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
 cause (19-21)]
4.1.19: God is the cause, because the actions of persons are seen to be fruitless [without divine dispensation].
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.20: No, because without human action there is no production of fruits.
{Objection sūtra}
4.1.21: Since [human action] is caused by that [God], [the objection] is not a [valid] reason.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

 fortuitously (22-24)]
4.1.22: Existence arises without a cause, because of the observation of the sharpness of thorns and the like.
{Objection sūtra}
4.1.23: Because the causeless itself has a cause, [things do] not [arise] without a cause.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}
4.1.24: There is no refutation, because cause and effect are different from each other.
{Established-conclusion sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.25: sarvam anityam, utpattivināśadharmakatvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.26: na, anityatānityatvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.27: tadanityatvamagnerdāhyaṃ vināśyānuvināśavat
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.28: nityasyāpratyākhyānam, yathopalabdhi vyavasthānāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.25: Everything is impermanent, because [all things] possess the nature of origination and destruction. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.26: No, because impermanence itself would be impermanent. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.27: The impermanence of that [impermanence] is like the combustible being destroyed along with the fire, or like a destroyable thing perishing along [with its cause]. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.28: The permanent is not to be denied, because of the establishment [of things] according to their apprehension. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.29: sarvaṃ nityam, pañcabhūtanityatvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.30: na, utpattivināśakāraṇopalabdheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.31: tallakṣaṇāvarodhādapratiṣedhaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.32: na, utpattitatkāraṇopalabdheḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.33: na, vyavasthānupapatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.29: Everything is permanent, because the five elements are permanent. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.30: No, because origination and the causes of destruction are observed. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.31: [The thesis is] not refuted, because the definition encompasses [these cases]. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.32: No, because origination and its causes are observed. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.33: No, because the arrangement [of things] would be inexplicable. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.34: sarvaṃ pṛthag, bhāvalakṣaṇapṛthaktvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.35: na, anekalakṣaṇairekabhāvaniṣpatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.36: lakṣaṇavyavasthānādevāpratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.34: Everything is separate, because entities are distinguished by their defining characteristics. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.35: No, because a single entity is produced from multiple defining characteristics. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.36: [The thesis is] not refuted, because of the establishment of [distinct] defining characteristics. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.37: sarvam abhāvo bhāveṣvitaretarābhāvasiddheḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.38: na, svabhāvasiddherbhāvānām
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.39: na svabhāvasiddhiḥ, āpekṣikatvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.40: vyāhatatvādayuktam
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.37: Everything is non-existent (<s>abhāva</s>), because the mutual non-existence of entities among themselves is established. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.38: No, because entities are established by their own nature (<s>svabhāva</s>). {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.39: There is no establishment by own nature, because [all things] are relative (<s>āpekṣika</s>). {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.40: This is untenable, because it is self-contradictory. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

4.1.41: The one-sided <s>Sāṃkhya</s> [doctrine] is not established, because of the impossibility and possibility of a cause. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.42: No, because the component parts of the cause do not exist [separately]. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.43: Since [the cause] is without parts, [the objection] is groundless. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.44: sadyaḥ kālāntare ca phalaniṣpatteḥ saṃśayaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.45: na sadyaḥ, kālāntaropabhogyatvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.46: kālāntareṇāniṣpattiheturvināśāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.47: prāṅniṣpattervṛkṣaphalavat tatsyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.48: nāsanna sanna sadasat, sadasatorvaidharmayāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.49: utpādavyayadarśanāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.50: buddhisiddhaṃ tu tadasat
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.51: āśrayavyatirekād vṛkṣaphalotpattivad ityahetuḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.52: prīterātmāśrayatvādapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.53: na putrapaśustrīparicchedahiraṇyānnādiphalanirddeśāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.54: tatsambandhāt phalaniṣpattesteṣu phalavadupacāraḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
 (44-54)]
4.1.44: There is doubt whether the fruit arises immediately or at a later time. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.45: Not immediately, because it is to be enjoyed at a later time. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.46: The cause of its non-arising at a later time is destruction [of the cause in the interim]. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.47: Like the fruit of a tree, it would [arise] before [the destruction of the cause]. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.48: [The fruit before arising] is neither non-existent, nor existent, nor both existent and non-existent, because of the difference in nature between existence and non-existence. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.49: [Its existence is established] because origination and destruction are observed. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.50: But that [pre-existent fruit] is non-existent, being established [only] by cognition. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.51: The [analogy of] the fruit of a tree arising from a different substratum is a fallacious reason. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.52: [The thesis is] not refuted, because pleasure has the self as its substratum. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.53: No, because [Vedic texts] specify fruits such as sons, cattle, women, retinue, gold, and food. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.54: Because the fruit arises in connection with those [external things], there is a figurative designation of fruit applied to them. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.55: vividhavādhanāyogād duḥkhameva janmotpattiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.56: na, sukhasyāpyantarālaniṣpatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.57: bhādhanānivṛttervedayataḥ pryeṣaṇadoṣādapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.58: duḥkhavikalpe sukhābhimānācca
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.55: Birth and existence are nothing but suffering (<s>duḥkha</s>), because of connection with various afflictions. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.56: No, because pleasure also arises in the intervals. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.57: [The thesis is] not refuted, because of the fault of seeking [pleasure], since the one who experiences [pleasure] has not ceased from affliction. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.58: And because of the false identification of pleasure in what is [actually] a modification of suffering. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.1.59: ṛṇakleśapravṛttyanubndhādapavargābhāvaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.60: pradhānaśabdānupapatterguṇaśabdenānuvādo nindāpraśaṃsopapatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.61: samāropaṇādātmanyapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.62: pātracayāntānupapatteśca phalābhāvaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.63: suṣuptasya svapnādarśane kleśābhāvādapavargaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.64: na pravṛttiḥ pratisandhānāya hīnakleśasya
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.65: na, kleśasantateḥ svābhāvikatvāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.1.66: prāgutpatterabhāvānityatvavat svābhāvike 'pyanityatvam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.67: aṇuśyāmatānityatvavad vā
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.1.68: na, saṅkalpanimittatvācca rāgādīnām
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.1.59: There is no liberation (<s>apavarga</s>), because of the bondage of debt, affliction, and activity. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.60: Since the term <s>pradhāna</s> (primary [cause]) is inapplicable [to the self], the [Vedic] reference by the term <s>guṇa</s> (quality) is a reiteration, because [the passage] is explicable as censure or praise. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.61: Due to superimposition [of qualities] upon the self, [the thesis is] not refuted. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.62: And because of the impossibility [of an infinite series] ending in a vessel-heap, there is absence of fruit [for the opponent]. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.63: Liberation [is established], because in deep sleep, due to the absence of dreams, there is absence of affliction. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.64: One whose afflictions are diminished does not engage [in activity] for the purpose of reconnection [with the body]. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.65: No, because the continuum of afflictions is natural. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.1.66: Just as there is impermanence in what was non-existent before its origination, so too there is impermanence even in what is natural. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.67: Or like the impermanence of the dark color of an atom. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.1.68: No, [afflictions are not natural,] because <s>rāga</s> (attachment) and the like arise from volition (<s>saṅkalpa</s>). {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
(dvitīyam āhnikam)
[tattvajñānotpattiprakaraṇam(1-3)]
4.2.1: doṣanimittānāṃ tattvajñānādahaṅkāranivṛttiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.2: doṣanimittaṃ rūpādayo viṣayaḥ saṅkalpakṛtāḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.3: tannimittaṃ tvavayavyabhimānaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
(Second lesson)
[Section on the arising of knowledge of reality (1-3)]
4.2.1: Through knowledge of reality regarding the causes of faults, there is the cessation of ego-sense (<s>ahaṅkāra</s>). {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.2: The causes of faults are the sense-objects such as form, etc., which are produced by volition. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.3: The cause of that [volition], however, is the false identification with the composite whole (<s>avayavin</s>). {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.2.4: vidyāvidyādvaividhyāt saṃśayaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.5: tadasaṃśayaḥ, pūrvahetuprasiddhatvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.6: vṛttyanupapatterapi tarhi na saṃśayaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.7: kṛtsnaikadeśāvṛttitvādavayavānāmavayavyabhāvaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.8: teṣu cāvṛtteravayavyabhāvaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.9: pṛthak cāvayavebhyo 'vṛtteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.10: na cāvayavyavayavāḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.11: ekasmin bhedābhāvād bhedaśabdaprayogānupapatterapraśnaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.12: avayavāntarabhāve 'pyavṛtterahetuḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.13: keśasamūhe taimirikopalabdhivat tadupalabdhiḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.14: svaviṣayānatikrameṇendriyasya paṭumandabhāvādviṣayagrahaṇasya tathābhābo nāviṣaye pravṛttiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.15: avayavāvayaviprasaṅgaścaivamāpralayāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.16: na pralayo 'ṇusadbhāvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.17: paraṃ vā truṭeḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.2.4: Doubt [arises], because of the twofold nature of knowledge and ignorance. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.5: There is no doubt about that, because the prior reason is well-established. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.6: Then there should be no doubt even because of the impossibility of occurrence. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.7: Because the parts do not exist in the whole or in a portion [of the whole], the composite whole does not exist. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.8: And because of the non-occurrence [of the whole] in them [the parts], the composite whole does not exist. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.9: And because of the non-occurrence [of the whole] separately from the parts. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.10: And the parts are not [themselves] composite wholes. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.11: Since in a single [entity] there is no difference, the use of the word "difference" is inexplicable — hence no question [arises]. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.12: Even when there exist sub-parts, the non-occurrence [argument] is a fallacious reason. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.13: Like the perception of one with double vision (<s>taimirika</s>) seeing [many] in a mass of hairs, so is that perception. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.14: Because the sense faculty, without exceeding its own domain, [operates] with sharpness or dullness, there is such [variation in] apprehension of objects — it does not operate on what is not its object. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.15: And thus [there would be] an infinite regress of parts and composite wholes, up to dissolution. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.16: There is no [total] dissolution, because atoms exist. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.17: Or [the regress terminates] at the ultimate indivisible unit (<s>truṭi</s>). {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.2.18: ākāśavyatibhedāt tadanupapattiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.19: ākāśāsarvagatatvaṃ vā
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.20: antarbahiśca kāryadravyasya kāraṇāntaravacanādakārye tadabhāvaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.21: śabdasaṃyogavibhāvācca sarvagatam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.22: avyūhāviṣṭambhavibhutvāni cākāśadharmāḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.23: mūrtimatāṃ ca saṃsthānopapatteravayavasadbhāvaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.24: saṃyogopapatteśca
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.25: anavasthākāritvādanavasthānupapatteścāpratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
 (18-25)]
4.2.18: Because of the interpenetration of space (<s>ākāśa</s>), that [composite whole] is impossible. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.19: Or space is not omnipresent. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.20: Since, in the effect-substance, a different cause [i.e., space] is stated to be both inside and outside, its absence in what is not an effect [follows]. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.21: And [space] is omnipresent, because of the apprehension of sound, contact, and vibration [everywhere]. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.22: Non-arrangement, non-resistance, and omnipresence are the properties of space (<s>ākāśa</s>). {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.23: And for material entities, the existence of parts follows from the possibility of spatial configuration. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.24: And from the possibility of conjunction. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.25: [The thesis is] not refuted, because it [merely] produces an infinite regress, and because such an infinite regress is impossible. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.2.26: buddhyā vivecanāttu bhāvānāṃ yāthātmyānupalabdhistantvapakarṣaṇe paṭasadbhāvānupalabdhivat tadanupalabdhiḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.27: vyāhatatvādahetuḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.28: tadāśrayatvādapṛthaggrahaṇam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.29: pramāṇataścārthapratipatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.30: pramāṇānupapattyupapattibhyām
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.31: svapnaviṣayābhimānavadayaṃ pramāṇaprameyābhimānaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.32: māyāgandharvanagaramṛgatṛṣṇikāvadvā
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.33: hetvabhāvādasiddhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.34: smṛtisaṃkalpavacca svapnaviṣayābhimānaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.35: mithyopalabdhervināśastattvajñānātsvapnaviṣayābhimānapraṇāśavat pratibodhe
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.36: buddheścaivaṃ nimittasadbhāvopalambhāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.37: tattvapradhānabhedācca mithyābuddherdvaividhyopapattiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.2.26: Through analysis by the intellect, the true nature of entities is not apprehended — like the non-apprehension of the existence of cloth when its threads are removed, so is that non-apprehension. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.27: [This is] a fallacious reason, because it is self-contradictory. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.28: Because [the parts] are the substratum of that [whole], they are not apprehended separately. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.29: And because the object is cognized through the means of valid knowledge (<s>pramāṇa</s>). {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.30: [This is established] by the impossibility and possibility of the means of valid knowledge. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.31: Like the false identification with dream-objects, this is a false identification with <s>pramāṇa</s> and <s>prameya</s>. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.32: Or like an illusion, a mirage-city, or a mirage. {<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>-sūtra}
4.2.33: [This is] not established, because of the absence of a [valid] reason. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.34: The false identification with dream-objects is like memory and imagination. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.35: The destruction of false perception [occurs] through knowledge of reality, like the destruction of the false identification with dream-objects upon awakening. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.36: And because a cause is apprehended even for cognition itself. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}
4.2.37: And because of the distinction between reality and mere appearance, the twofold nature of false cognition is explicable. {<s>siddhānta</s>-sūtra}

body.text:
4.2.38: samādhiviśeṣābhyāsāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.39: na, arthaviśeṣaprāvalyāt
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.40: kṣudādibhiḥ pravartanācca
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.41: pūrvakṛtaphalānubandhāt tadutpattiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.42: araṇyaguhāpulinādiṣu yogābhyāsopadeśaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.43: apavarge 'pyevaṃ prasaṅgaḥ
{pūrvapakṣa-sūtra}
4.2.44: na, niṣpannāvaśyambhāvitvāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.45: tadabhāvaścāpavarge
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.46: tadarthaṃ yamaniyamābhyāsātmasaṃskāro yogāccādhyātmavidhyupāyaiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.47: jñānagrahaṇābhyāsastadvidyaiśca saha saṃvādaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.48: taṃ śiṣyagurusabrahmacāriviśiṣṭaśreyo 'rthibhiranasūyibhirabhyupeyāt
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.49: pratipakṣahīnam api vā prayojanārthamarthitve

body.translated_text:
4.2.38: [Knowledge of reality grows] from the practice of a particular kind of <s>samādhi</s> (meditative concentration).
{Established position (<s>siddhānta</s>)}
4.2.39: [Objection:] No, because of the predominance of particular objects [which distract the mind].
{Objection (<s>pūrvapakṣa</s>)}
4.2.40: And because of being impelled by hunger and the like.
{Objection}
4.2.41: [Reply:] The arising of those [hunger etc.] is due to the connection with the fruits of actions previously performed.
{Established position}
4.2.42: The instruction [is] to practice <s>yoga</s> (meditative discipline) in forests, caves, sand-banks, and the like.
{Established position}
4.2.43: [Objection:] Even in <s>apavarga</s> (liberation) there would be the same absurd consequence [of being affected by hunger etc.].
{Objection}
4.2.44: [Reply:] No, because [at that stage one has] completed what must necessarily [be completed].
{Established position}
4.2.45: And in <s>apavarga</s> (liberation), there is the absence of that [connection with prior <s>karma</s>].
{Established position}
4.2.46: For that purpose, the purification of the self through the practice of <s>yama</s> (restraints) and <s>niyama</s> (observances), and through <s>yoga</s> — by means of the methods of spiritual knowledge (<s>adhyātmavidyā</s>).
{Established position}
4.2.47: The practice of grasping knowledge, and discussion with those learned in that knowledge.
{Established position}
4.2.48: One should approach that [knowledge] through [associations with] pupils, teachers, fellow celibate students (<s>sabrahmacārin</s>), and those who seek the highest good and are free from envy.
{Established position}
4.2.49: Or even [a discussion] lacking an opponent, when there is desire for meaning, for the sake of the purpose [of gaining knowledge].

body.text:
4.2.50: tattvādhyavasāyasaṃrakṣaṇārthaṃ jalpavitaṇḍe, bījaprarohasaṃrakṣṇārthaṃ kaṇṭakaśākhāvaraṇavat
{siddhānta-sūtra}
4.2.51: tābhyāṃ vigṛhyakathanam
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
4.2.50: <s>Jalpa</s> (contentious debate) and <s>vitaṇḍā</s> (caviling) [serve] for the preservation of the ascertainment of truth — just as the protection of a thorny hedge [serves] for the preservation of a seed's sprouting.
{Established position}
4.2.51: Disputatious debate (<s>vigṛhyakathana</s>) [is conducted] through those two [<s>jalpa</s> and <s>vitaṇḍā</s>].
{Established position}

body.text:
(atha pañcamaḥ adhyāyaḥ)
(prathamam āhnikam)
[satpratipakṣadeśanābhāsaprakarṇam(1-3)]
5.1.1: sādharmyavaidharmyotkarṣāpakarṣavarṇyāvarṇyavikalpasādhyaprāpty aprāptipraṣaṅgapratidṛṛṣṭāntānutpattisaṃśayaprakaraṇahetvarthā pattyaviśeṣopapattyupalabdhyanupalabdhinityānityakaāryasamāḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.2: sādharmyavaidharmyābhyāmupasaṃhāre taddharmaviparyayopapatteḥ sādharmyavaidharmyasamau
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.3: gotvād gosiddhivat tatsiddhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
(Now the fifth chapter.)
(First lesson.)
[Section on Genuine Pseudo-Refutations and Spurious Ones (sūtras 1-3)]
5.1.1: [The pseudo-refutations (<s>jāti</s>) are:] based on similarity (<s>sādharmya</s>), based on dissimilarity (<s>vaidharmya</s>), based on superiority (<s>utkarṣa</s>), based on inferiority (<s>apakarṣa</s>), based on describability (<s>varṇya</s>), based on indescribability (<s>āvarṇya</s>), based on alternative (<s>vikalpa</s>), based on achievability (<s>sādhyaprāpti</s>), based on non-achievability (<s>aprāpti</s>), based on undesirable consequence (<s>prasaṅga</s>), based on counter-example (<s>pratidṛṣṭānta</s>), based on non-origination (<s>anutpatti</s>), based on doubt (<s>saṃśaya</s>), based on topic (<s>prakaraṇa</s>), based on the reason (<s>hetu</s>), based on implication (<s>arthāpatti</s>), based on non-distinction (<s>aviśeṣa</s>), based on logical possibility (<s>upapatti</s>), based on apprehension (<s>upalabdhi</s>), based on non-apprehension (<s>anupalabdhi</s>), based on impermanence (<s>anitya</s>), based on permanence (<s>nitya</s>), and based on the effect (<s>kārya</s>) — these are the equal [counter-arguments].
{Established position}
5.1.2: In the subsumption through similarity and dissimilarity, because of the logical possibility of the reversal of that property — the similarity-equal and dissimilarity-equal [are refuted].
{Established position}
5.1.3: Its [the thesis'] establishment is like the establishment of a cow from cow-ness.
{Established position}

[Section on the Six Pseudo-Refutations Beginning with Superiority-Equal (sūtras 4-6)]
5.1.4: Because of the alternative in the properties of the <s>sādhya</s> (probandum) and the example, and because both require to be proved — the superiority-equal (<s>utkarṣasama</s>), inferiority-equal (<s>apakarṣasama</s>), describability-equal (<s>varṇyasama</s>), and alternative-equal (<s>vikalpasama</s>) [are refuted together with the achievability-equal (<s>sādhyasama</s>)].
{Established position}
5.1.5: Because of the establishment of subsumption from some similarity, the non-refutation [follows] from dissimilarity.
{Established position}
5.1.6: And because of the logical possibility of the example through the attribution of the <s>sādhya</s> (probandum).
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.7: prāpya sādhyamaprāpya vā hetoḥ prāptyāviśiṣṭatvādaprāptyāsādhāka tvācca prāptyaprāptisamau
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.8: ghaṭādiniṣpattidarśanāt pīḍane cābhicārādapratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.7: Whether the reason (<s>hetu</s>) has reached the <s>sādhya</s> (probandum) or not — because it is not distinguished from [the situation of] reaching, or because, by not reaching, it fails to prove — [these are] the achievability-equal (<s>prāptisama</s>) and non-achievability-equal (<s>aprāptisama</s>).
{Established position}
5.1.8: Because of the observation of the production of pots and the like, and because of [counter-examples of] pressure and magical rites — there is no refutation.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.9: dṛṣṭāntasya kāraṇānapadeśāt pratyavasthānācca pratidṛṣṭāntena prasaṅgapratidṛṣṭāntasamau
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.10: pradīpopādānaprasaṅganivṛttivat tadvinivṛttiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.11: pratidṛṣṭāntahetutve ca nāheturdṛṣṭāntaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.9: Because of the non-indication of a cause for the example, and because of the counter-argument [made] through a counter-example — [these are] the undesirable-consequence-equal (<s>prasaṅgasama</s>) and counter-example-equal (<s>pratidṛṣṭāntasama</s>).
{Established position}
5.1.10: The reversal of that [undesirable consequence is possible], just as [one avoids] the undesirable consequence of accepting a lamp [as eternal because it was lit].
{Established position}
5.1.11: And the counter-example, when it serves as a reason, is not a non-reason [merely because it is] an example.
{Established position}

5.1.12: The non-origination-equal (<s>anutpattisama</s>) [argues:] because of the absence of a cause prior to origination.
{Established position}
5.1.13: Because of the logical possibility of a cause for what has thus originated, there is no refutation of the cause.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.14: sāmānyadṛṣṭāntayoraindriyakatve samāne nityānityasādharmyāt saṃśayasamaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.15: sādharmyātsaṃśaye na saṃśayo vaidharmyādubhayathā vā saṃśaye 'tyanta saṃśayaprasaṅgo nityatvānabhyūpagamācca sāmānyasyāpratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.14: The doubt-equal (<s>saṃśayasama</s>) [argues:] because of the similarity in being beyond the senses between the common property and the example, [and] from the similarity with permanent and impermanent [things].
{Established position}
5.1.15: Because, from similarity, there is doubt — but there is no [further] doubt in [a case of] doubt; [and because] from dissimilarity [there is also doubt], or [because] in both ways there would be the absurd consequence of perpetual doubt; and because the universal (<s>sāmānya</s>) is not denied due to the non-acceptance of permanence — there is no refutation.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.16: ubhayasādharmyāt prakriyāsiddheḥ prakaraṇasamaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.17: pratipakṣāt prakaraṇasiddheḥ pratiṣedhānupapattiḥ pratipakṣopapatteḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.16: The topic-equal (<s>prakaraṇasama</s>) [argues:] because of the non-establishment of the procedure due to similarity with both [sides].
{Established position}
5.1.17: Because from the counter-thesis the procedure is established, the refutation is untenable [and] because of the logical possibility of the counter-thesis.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.18: traikālyāsiddherhetorahetusamaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.19: na hetutaḥ sādhyasiddhestraikālyāssiddhiḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.20: pratiṣedhānupapatteśca pratiṣeddhavyāpratiṣedhaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.18: The reason-equal (<s>ahetusama</s>) [argues:] because of the non-establishment of the reason in the three times.
{Established position}
5.1.19: Not from the reason [alone comes] the establishment of the <s>sādhya</s> (probandum) in all three times.
{Established position}
5.1.20: And because the refutation is untenable, there is no refutation of what is to be refuted.
{Established position}

5.1.21: The implication-equal (<s>arthāpattisama</s>) [argues:] because of the establishment of the counter-thesis through implication (<s>arthāpatti</s>).
{Established position}
5.1.22: Because of the implication of what is unstated, the abandonment of the thesis is logically possible; [but] because what is unstated is inconclusive — there is no [valid] implication.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.23: ekadharmopapatteraviśeṣe sarvāviśeṣaprasaṅgātsadbhābopapatter-
aviśeṣasamaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.24: kvaciddharmānupapatteḥ kvaciccopapatteḥ pratiṣedhābhāvaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.23: The non-distinction-equal (<s>aviśeṣasama</s>) [argues:] because of the logical possibility of a single property [in all things], if there is non-distinction, there would be the absurd consequence of universal non-distinction; and because of the logical possibility of existence.
{Established position}
5.1.24: Because in some cases the property is inapplicable and in some cases applicable, there is no absence of refutation.
{Established position}

5.1.25: The logical-possibility-equal (<s>upapattisama</s>) [argues:] because of the logical possibility of a cause for both.
{Established position}
5.1.26: Because the cause of logical possibility is not shaken — there is no refutation.
{Established position}

5.1.27: The apprehension-equal (<s>upalabdhisama</s>) [argues:] because even in the absence of the indicated cause, there is apprehension.
{Established position}
5.1.28: Because of the logical possibility of that property from another cause as well — there is no refutation.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.29: tadanupalabdheranupalambhādabhāvasiddhau tadviparītopapatter anupalabdhisamaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.30: anupalambhātmakatvādanupalabdherahetuḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.31: jñānavikalpānāṃ ca bhāvābhāvasaṃvedanādadhyātmam
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.29: The non-apprehension-equal (<s>anupalabdhisama</s>) [argues:] because of the non-apprehension of the non-apprehension of that [cause], in the establishment of [its] absence, there is the logical possibility of the opposite.
{Established position}
5.1.30: Because non-apprehension is of the nature of non-perception, it is an inconclusive reason.
{Established position}
5.1.31: And because of the cognizing of the existence and non-existence of the varieties of knowledge — within the self (<s>adhyātman</s>).
{Established position}

body.text:
[anityasamaprakaraṇam(32-34)
5.1.32: sādharmyāttulyadharmopapatteḥ sarvānityatvaprasaṅgādanityasamaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.33: sādharmyādasiddheḥ pratiṣedhāsiddhiḥ pratiṣedhyasādharmyācca
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.34: dṛṣṭānte ca sādhyasādhanabhāvena prajñātasya dharmasya tasysa cobhayathā bhāvānnāviśeṣaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
[Section on the Impermanence-Equal (sūtras 32-34)]
5.1.32: The impermanence-equal (<s>anityasama</s>) [argues:] because of the logical possibility of a property of equal status from similarity, there would be the absurd consequence of universal impermanence.
{Established position}
5.1.33: Because the establishment [of impermanence] from similarity is not achieved, the establishment of the refutation is not achieved; and because of the similarity of what is to be refuted [with the refutation].
{Established position}
5.1.34: And because the property that is recognized as having the relation of <s>sādhya</s> (probandum) and <s>sādhana</s> (means of proof) in the example exists in both ways — there is no non-distinction.
{Established position}

5.1.35: The permanence-equal (<s>nityasama</s>) [argues:] because of the logical possibility of permanence in what is impermanent, since permanent-impermanent [are] in [the nature of] the impermanent.
{Established position}
5.1.36: Because in what is to be refuted, permanent-impermanent [are] in [the nature of] the impermanent, and because of the logical possibility of impermanence — there is no absence of refutation.
{Established position}

5.1.37: The effect-equal (<s>kāryasama</s>) [argues:] because of the multiplicity of the effects of effort.
{Established position}
5.1.38: If the effect is different [from what is claimed], [then] effort would not be the cause — because of the logical possibility of a cause for [its] non-apprehension.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.1.39: pratiṣedhe 'pi samānadoṣaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.40: sarvatraivam
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.41: pratiṣedhavipratiṣedhe pratiṣedhadoṣavad doṣaḥ
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.42: pratiṣedhaṃ sadoṣamabhyupetya pratiṣedhavipratiṣedhe samāno doṣaprasaṅgo matānujñā
{siddhānta-sūtra}
5.1.43: svapakṣalakṣanāpekṣopapattyupasaṃhāre hetunirdeśe parapakṣadoṣā bhyupagamāt samāno doṣa iti
{siddhānta-sūtra}

body.translated_text:
5.1.39: Even in the refutation, the same defect [applies].
{Established position}
5.1.40: This [applies] everywhere in the same way.
{Established position}
5.1.41: In the counter-refutation of the refutation, the defect is [the same] as the defect of the refutation.
{Established position}
5.1.42: Having accepted the refutation as defective, in the counter-refutation of the refutation, because of the absurd consequence of the same defect — [this amounts to] the concession of the [opponent's] position (<s>matānujñā</s>).
{Established position}
5.1.43: When the subsumption is made with reference to the characteristic of one's own position and through logical possibility, [and] the defect of the opponent's position is accepted through the indication of the reason — the defect is the same.
{Established position}

body.text:
5.2.2: pratidṛṣṭāntadharmābhyanujñā svadṛṣṭānte pratijñāhāniḥ
{pratijñāhāniḥ lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.3: pratijñātārthapratiṣedhadharmavikalpāt tadarthanirdeśaḥ pratijñāntara.
{pratijñāntara-lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.4: pratijñāhetvorvirodhaḥ pratijñāvirodhaḥ
{pratijñāvirodhaḥ lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.5: pakṣapratiṣedhe pratijñātārthāpanayanaṃ pratijñāsannyāsaḥ
{pratijñāsannyāsaḥ lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.6: aviśeṣokte hetau pratiṣiddhe viśeṣamicchato hetvantaram
{hetvantaram lakṣaṇa-sūtram}

body.translated_text:
5.2.2: The acceptance of a property [asserted] in the opponent's example [which leads to the defeat of] one's own example constitutes abandonment of the thesis (<s>pratijñāhāni</s>).
{Definition of abandonment of thesis}
5.2.3: The statement of an alternative meaning, due to the alternation between the denial of the thesis and the property [asserted], is a shift of thesis (<s>pratijñāntara</s>).
{Definition of shift of thesis}
5.2.4: The contradiction between the thesis and the reason is self-contradiction of the thesis (<s>pratijñāvirodha</s>).
{Definition of self-contradiction of thesis}
5.2.5: When the counter-thesis is denied, the withdrawal of the meaning of the thesis is renunciation of the thesis (<s>pratijñāsannyāsa</s>).
{Definition of renunciation of thesis}
5.2.6: When a generally stated reason is refuted, seeking a specific [reason] constitutes a shift of reason (<s>hetvantara</s>).
{Definition of shift of reason}

body.text:
5.2.7: prakṛtādarthādapratisambddhārthamarthāntaram
{arthāntaram lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.8: varṇakramanirdeśavannirarthakam
{nirarthakam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.9: pariṣatprativādibhyāṃ trirabhihitamapyavijñātamavijñātārtham
{avijñātārtham lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.10: paurvāparyāyogādapratisambaddhārthamapārthakam
{apārthakam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}

body.translated_text:
5.2.7: A meaning unconnected with the subject under discussion is an irrelevant topic (<s>arthāntara</s>).
{Definition of irrelevant topic}
5.2.8: What is meaningless (<s>nirarthaka</s>) is like stating the order of letters [without meaning].
{Definition of meaninglessness}
5.2.9: What is not understood even after being stated three times to the assembly and the opponent is unintelligible (<s>avijñātārtha</s>).
{Definition of the unintelligible}
5.2.10: What is incoherent (<s>apārthaka</s>), having an unconnected meaning due to the absence of [proper] sequence.
{Definition of the incoherent}

body.text:
5.2.11: avayavaviparyāsavacanamaprāptakālam
{aprāptakālam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.12: hīnamanyatamenāpyavayavena nyūnam
{nyūnam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.13: hetūdāharaṇādhikam adhikam
{adhikam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}

body.translated_text:
5.2.11: Stating the members [of the syllogism] in a wrong order is untimeliness (<s>aprāptakāla</s>).
{Definition of untimeliness}
5.2.12: What is deficient (<s>nyūna</s>) is lacking even one of the members [of the syllogism].
{Definition of deficiency}
5.2.13: What is excessive (<s>adhika</s>) is [a syllogism] with an additional reason or example beyond [what is needed].
{Definition of excess}

body.text:
5.2.14: śabdārthayoḥ punarvacanaṃ punaruktam anyatra anuvādāt
{punaruktam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.15: anuvāde tu apunaruktaṃ śabdābhyāsāt arthaviśeṣopapatteḥ
{punaruktam lakṣaṇāpavādasūtram}
5.2.16: arthāt āpannasya svaśabdena punarvacanam
{punaruktam lakṣaṇāpavādasūtram}

body.translated_text:
5.2.14: The re-statement of either words or meanings, except in the case of [legitimate] restatement (<s>anuvāda</s>), is tautology (<s>punarukta</s>).
{Definition of tautology}
5.2.15: In the case of restatement (<s>anuvāda</s>), however, it is not tautology, because through verbal repetition a specific meaning is possible.
{Exception to the definition of tautology}
5.2.16: Re-stating in one's own words what is already arrived at from the meaning.
{Exception to the definition of tautology}

body.text:
5.2.17: vijñātasya pariṣadā triḥ abhihitasya api apratyuccāraṇam ananubhāṣaṇam
{ananubhāṣaṇam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.18: avijñātam ca ajñānam
{ajñānam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.19: uttarasya apratipattiḥ apratibhā
{apratibhā lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.20: kāryavyāsaṅgāt kathāvicchedaḥ vikṣepaḥ
{vikṣepaḥ lakṣaṇa-sūtram}

body.translated_text:
5.2.17: Not repeating what has been understood by the assembly, even after it has been stated three times, is non-repetition (<s>ananubhāṣaṇa</s>).
{Definition of non-repetition}
5.2.18: And what is not understood constitutes ignorance (<s>ajñāna</s>).
{Definition of ignorance}
5.2.19: The failure to produce a response constitutes lack of ready wit (<s>apratibhā</s>).
{Definition of lack of ready wit}
5.2.20: Breaking off the discussion due to engagement in [other] activity is evasion (<s>vikṣepa</s>).
{Definition of evasion}

body.text:
5.2.21: svapakṣe doṣābhyupagamāt parapakṣe doṣaprasaṅgaḥ matānujñā
{matānujñā lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.22: nigrahasthānaprāptasya anigrahaḥ paryanuyojyopekṣaṇam
{paryanuyojyopekṣaṇam lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.23: anigrahasthāne nigrahasthānābhiyogaḥ niranuyojyānuyogaḥ
{niranuyojyānuyoga lakṣaṇa-sūtram}

body.translated_text:
5.2.21: The admission of a fault in one's own position, [leading to] the consequence of a fault in the opponent's position, is self-incriminating admission (<s>matānujñā</s>).
{Definition of self-incriminating admission}
5.2.22: Not censuring one who has incurred a point of defeat is overlooking the censurable (<s>paryanuyojyopekṣaṇa</s>).
{Definition of overlooking the censurable}
5.2.23: Charging with a point of defeat where no point of defeat exists is censuring the uncensurable (<s>niranuyojyānuyoga</s>).
{Definition of censuring the uncensurable}

body.text:
5.2.24: siddhāntam abhyupetya aniyamāt kathāprasaṅgaḥ apasiddhāntaḥ
{apasiddhānta lakṣaṇa-sūtram}
5.2.25: hetvābhāsāḥ ca yathoktāḥ
{hetvābhāsa nirūpaṇam}

body.translated_text:
5.2.24: Having accepted an established conclusion, if debate ensues [because of] its non-determinateness, that is a pseudo-established conclusion (<s>apasiddhānta</s>).
{Definition of pseudo-established conclusion}
5.2.25: And the fallacious reasons (<s>hetvābhāsa</s>) are as stated [previously].
{Specification of fallacious reasons}
